{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"alias":"camerashy-erectable-frog","creation_channel":"cli","description":"## Context / Problem\nWhen a compaction sweep holds the store write lock, a blocked session cannot tell what is\nholding it or whether interrupting it is safe. Both facts are already known to the code and\nneither reaches the operator.\n\nFrom a live incident on 2026-08-13, reported by a session in a different lane\n(`vigilant-erasable-pony`): a sweep held `.tickets-tracker/.ticket-write.lock` for 24+ minutes\nat 0.0% CPU. What that session saw was:\n\n    flock: could not acquire lock after 181s (holder: host=... pid=... held=47s)\n\nA bare pid. Identifying it as compaction required `lsof` and `ps`. A second sweep, spawned by a\ndifferent worktree's close, blocked the same session again minutes later — the blast radius is\nhost-wide across worktrees, so \"check your own checkout\" does not help either.\n\nThe information gap is the whole problem, because the answer is already documented:\n`compact_trigger.run_sweep` guarantees \"a skipped sweep is a no-op for correctness (the events\nstay live and the next trigger folds them)\". An operator holding that guarantee can safely\nSIGTERM the holder. An operator looking at an unattributed pid cannot distinguish it from a\nmystery hang mid-write, and the safe response to that is to wait — which is exactly wrong here.\nOne sweep was ultimately killed manually (pid 98440, 26 minutes) to unblock three agents.\n\n## Approach\nSay what the holder is, and say that it is interruptible — at the two places a blocked writer\nalready looks.\n\n**1. Label the lock holder.** `lock_owner._owner_stamp` writes the v2 ownership line\n(`rebar-lock v2 host=... ns=... pid=... start=...`) that `describe_lock_holder` renders into\nevery `LockTimeout`. Add an optional operation label to that stamp, set by the sweep, so the\ntimeout reads `... pid=98440 held=181s op=compact-sweep` instead of a bare pid.\n\nThe v2 format has a documented forward-compatibility contract that must be preserved exactly:\nthe line is deliberately COLON-FREE so an older rebar parsing it with the legacy\n`host, sep, pid = stamp.partition(\":\")` finds no separator and declines to act\n(`_store/lock.py` module docstring, bug castoff-tigerseye-ammonite). A new `key=value` field\nkeeps that property, and `_parse_v2_stamp` already tolerates unknown fields, so an older reader\nignores it rather than misparsing. The label is descriptive only — it must never influence\nreclamation, exactly as the existing fields do not.\n\n**2. State the remedy in the error.** When the holder is labelled as optional housekeeping,\n`LockTimeout`'s message should say so and name the safe action, rather than leaving the reader\nto find a docstring in `compact_trigger`. Something an operator can act on without a code\nsearch: that the holder is a compaction sweep, that interrupting it loses no data, and that the\nnext trigger re-folds.\n\nDeliberately NOT proposed here: auto-killing a long-running sweep. That is a policy decision\nabout pre-empting one process from another, it needs its own justification, and the reported\nincidents were all resolved by a human with the right information. Give the human the\ninformation first.\n\n## Scope\n- `src/rebar/_store/lock_owner.py` — optional `op=` field in the v2 stamp, colon-free, ignored\n  by older readers, never consulted by staleness logic.\n- `src/rebar/_store/lock.py` — render the label in `describe_lock_holder`; extend the\n  `LockTimeout` message with the safe-to-interrupt remedy when the holder is labelled optional.\n- `src/rebar/_commands/compact_trigger.py`, `src/rebar/_commands/compact.py` — set the label\n  around the sweep's lock acquisitions.\n- `tests/unit/test_lock_holder_labelling.py` — new tests.\n\n## Testing\n- A labelled holder appears in `describe_lock_holder` output and in the `LockTimeout` message.\n- An UNLABELLED holder renders exactly as today (no format change for ordinary writers).\n- The stamp stays colon-free with the label present, so the documented legacy-reader refusal\n  still holds.\n- `_parse_v2_stamp` accepts a stamp carrying an unknown extra field, proving forward\n  compatibility in the other direction.\n- Reclamation is unaffected: a labelled stale lock and an unlabelled stale lock are treated\n  identically by `stamped_file_is_stale`.\n- A sweep's real lock acquisition carries the label end to end.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] A blocked writer's `LockTimeout` names the holder as a compaction sweep — a test asserts\n      the message contains the operation label rather than only a pid.\n- [ ] That message states the interruption is safe and lossless — a test asserts the remedy\n      text is present for a labelled optional holder and ABSENT for an ordinary writer.\n- [ ] An unlabelled holder's rendering is byte-identical to today's — a test pins the existing\n      format so ordinary writers see no change.\n- [ ] The stamp remains colon-free with a label, and `_parse_v2_stamp` ignores unknown fields —\n      tests assert both directions of the forward-compat contract.\n- [ ] Staleness and reclamation decisions ignore the label entirely — a test asserts a labelled\n      and an unlabelled stale lock are adjudicated identically.\n- [ ] The full local gate set plus CI's pytest tiers pass.","id":"301d-0769-ed02-42f6","parent_id":"","priority":2,"tags":[],"ticket_type":"bug","title":"Lock timeouts name a bare pid, not the compaction sweep holding it"},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"CREATE","timestamp":1786655926821684001,"uuid":"47c7d5b6-fa2e-48d9-9f5c-de9a8a5d89d7"}